        ZmanMethod("polar-start-of-day-teshuvos-vehanhagos", "twilight", "Polar Start Of Day Teshuvot Ve Hanhagot", false, false, false, true, false, explicitElevation = false, sourceMethod = "getPolarStartOfDayTeshuvosVehanhagos", hebrewLabel = "Polar Start Of Day תשובות והנהגות", definition = "A polar-only day boundary. Uses solar midnight during continuous daylight, or solar noon during continuous darkness with no 16.1° dawn. Other dates have no result for this method.", sourceDefinition = "In Polar regions during the period where the sun is above the horizon 24-hours a day, Rav Moshe Sternbuch in מועדים וזמנים ח״ב ס׳ קנ״ה הע׳ א׳ ( ח״ד ס׳ ת״א הע׳ א׳ in the new edition) and in תשובות והנהגות ח״א ס׳ שט״ו, and ח״ה ס׳ פ״ד ruled that during the Polar summer night-based mitzvos are not applicable, and during the Polar winter, day-based mitzvos are not applicable. Despite this, the day of the calendar week changes when the sun is at its lowest position (an instantaneous set and rise) at getChatzosHalayla() in the Polar summer and when it is at its highest position in the Polar winter that occurs at getChatzosHayom(). Rabbi Dovid Heber the author of the שערי זמנים who discusses the Moadim Uzmanim's opinion clarified to me that in the Polar winter this really only applies when there is no alos hashachar . The lack of alos hashachar only occurs in Antarctica and in uninhabited extreme northern regions. This means almost every northern inhabited location has an alos hashachar and \"day\" even on December 21st. This method considers alos to be getAlos16Point1Degrees(). It should be noted that in the מועדים וזמנים linked above that was published in 1961, Rav Moshe Sternbuch mentions that [ ולתפלה, היום מתהלק, עד י״ב שעות שחרית, ומאז מנחה, ומעריב מתפלל סמוך ללילה כרבי יהודה כנל״ד ... ]. This comment (in () in the original, and [] in the new edition published in 2023) was not brought down in the תשובות והנהגות vol. 1 that was published in 1977 nor in vol. 5. published in 2009. Since it is not clear to me if this constitutes a retraction, the calculations for the start of mincha mentioned in the Moadim Uzmanim was omitted. This is 12 hours after the start of the day (either getChatzosHalayla() in the Polar summer, or getChatzosHayom() in the Polar winter. getPolarPlagHaminchaTeshuvosVehanhagos() was included and is the start of the time that maariv can be recited (until the end of the Polar day). was omitted. plag hamincha were not implemented. For those who want the zmanim, the end of Shacharis is essentially 12 hours after the start of the day (either getChatzosHalayla() in the Polar summer, or getChatzosHayom() in the Polar winter, while mincha will start at that point and can in daven mincha until night (or plag). FIXME: Attempt to clarify the discrepancy. Research if an earlier alos such as 18° should be used Consider logic for day with EITHER an alos or a sunset") { c -> c.getPolarStartOfDayTeshuvosVehanhagos() },
        ZmanMethod("polar-plag-hamincha-teshuvos-vehanhagos", "plag", "Polar Plag Teshuvot Ve Hanhagot \u00b7 Day: Previous polar day boundary \u2192 Current polar day boundary", false, false, false, true, true, explicitElevation = false, sourceMethod = "getPolarPlagHaminchaTeshuvosVehanhagos", hebrewLabel = "Polar פלג המנחה תשובות והנהגות", definition = "Uses 10.75 temporal hours of the 24-hour interval ending at the polar day boundary. The library’s supported half-day policy can affect the calculation.", sourceDefinition = "In Polar regions during the period where the sun is above the horizon 24-hours a day, Rav Moshe Sternbuch in מועדים וזמנים ח״ב ס׳ קנ״ה הע׳ א׳ ( ח״ד ס׳ ת״א הע׳ א׳ in the new edition) mentions the concept of zmanim based on his getPolarStartOfDayTeshuvosVehanhagos(). This calculates Instant, boolean) as 10.75 sha'os zmaniyos starting 24 hours prior to the current start/end of the current day. This will be very close to 2.5 clock hours before the start/end of the day.") { c -> c.getPolarPlagHaminchaTeshuvosVehanhagos() },
